{
  "id": "COMPOUND:218 - DISEASE:1932",
  "verbalization": "Enflurane, a volatile compound, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Macrostomia through its interaction with the coiled-coil domain containing 33 (CCD33) protein. This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, all of which indicate that enflurane affects CCD33, which in turn leads to Macrostomia. The evidence suggests that CCD33 plays a role in the development of Macrostomia, possibly through mechanisms involving cellular dysfunction or signaling pathways that are modulated by enflurane. The consistent involvement of CCD33 across different conditions, including Hypophosphatemic rickets, Melanoma-Pancreatic cancer syndrome, and Glaucoma 1, open angle, O, highlights the potential for enflurane to influence multiple physiological processes, potentially through shared molecular targets. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that enflurane may exert a role in the development of Macrostomia by modulating CCD33 function, though further experimental validation is required to establish the precise mechanism.",
